query:
I am hosting a chocolate tasting next month and want an affordable lineup, but I also want the tasting to teach my guests what the products actually are rather than repeat package slogans. I need an evidence audit and a practical purchase-and-tasting procedure, not an immediate brand recommendation.

The frozen shopping set contains five listings. Green & Black's is $22.98 for a variety pack whose title and detail describe eight 3.17-ounce bars spanning 85-percent dark, 70-percent dark, milk, milk with almonds and white, while Product Dimensions reports only 3.17 ounces and the selected flavor field says 70-percent cacao. Lindt white is $5.39 for one bar, with no mass shown. Lindt Classic milk is $9.58 and is titled 4.4 ounces and two pieces, while Product Dimensions reports 4.4 ounces. Ritter Sport is $7.99 for one 61-percent, 100-gram bar and is rated 55 percent over four reviews. Chocolove ruby is $8.99, is titled as two bars and selected as a 3.1-ounce two-pack, while Product Dimensions reports 3.1 ounces; it is rated 78 percent over twelve reviews.

The background packet discusses beans, nibs, chocolate liquor, cocoa butter, conching, white chocolate and the disputed ruby category. Three community pages contain only a planned local blind comparison of chocolate-covered caramels, place-specific hot-cocoa preferences and a lay discussion of how smell, texture, color and temperature contribute to flavor. The packet does not provide current delivered labels, complete ingredients and allergens for every offer, a matched tasting of these exact bars or a universal percentage-to-quality rule.

Please audit what the frozen seller, concept and community material can and cannot establish, then design a small, affordable and reversible tasting. Apply every boundary below:

- keep all titles SKUs prices ratings review counts pack sizes masses percentages origin certification process flavor and quality wording at frozen seller assertion scope;
- retain the Green Black eight times 3.17 ounce title versus 3.17 ounce Product Dimensions conflict and the selected 70 percent flavor field;
- retain the Lindt milk two piece 4.4 ounce and Chocolove two bar 3.1 ounce total mass ambiguities;
- show only conditional price per bar or declared mass arithmetic with pack and mass assumptions visible;
- do not rank the mixed pack white milk milk almond dark and ruby products as one homogeneous percentage or quality ladder;
- explain the general bean ferment dry roast nib liquor solids butter and finished chocolate chain without assigning an exact offer history;
- treat conching as equipment batch mixing particle volatile acid moisture texture and flavor context not as a longer is always better rule;
- distinguish chocolate liquor from alcohol and do not invent a formula from percentage or category name;
- keep white chocolate real or fake claims at cocoa component market definition and preference scope;
- keep ruby chocolate at a disputed category and marketing claim boundary and do not verify the Chocolove formula from its title;
- keep every forum statement at its author product place time and preference scope;
- verify current exact SKU variant pack mass count price shipping ingredients allergens cocoa components market lot date storage and return terms;
- screen guest allergies dietary rules and cross contact before selection or serving;
- separate category exploration from any like for like price or quality comparison;
- mask brand package price and marketing randomize equal coded portions balance order and standardize temperature where products are comparable;
- record aroma sweetness bitterness acidity texture melt aftertaste detected difference and overall liking separately;
- predeclare guest count budget lineup cost category coverage acceptance and identity allergen package and sensory stops; and
- choose the lowest commitment exact lineup passing identity allergen budget category coverage and local acceptance gates otherwise use a verified control reduce or defer.

Do not assume any of these possible approaches is correct. Compare and test their reasoning:

1. buy the Green Black pack because its eight bar title automatically proves the cheapest complete tasting;
2. build the lineup from the Lindt white Lindt milk Ritter dark and Chocolove ruby pages as if pack masses and labels were verified;
3. rank the bars by cocoa percentage as a direct measure of quality bitterness or guest preference;
4. call white chocolate fake because it lacks nonfat cocoa solids;
5. call ruby chocolate a verified fourth natural type and treat the Chocolove title as formula proof;
6. assume longer conching or more processing always means better taste and a higher justified price; or
7. verify exact current packages and guest constraints then run a small category aware blinded tasting and choose the lowest commitment passing lineup or control reduce or defer.

Start with an exact-offer matrix that retains the literal product, pack, mass, price, percentage, category, rating, review-count and unknown fields. Show any useful price-per-bar or price-per-declared-mass arithmetic, but label each assumption and do not resolve the three quantity conflicts from arithmetic alone.

Next, explain the general route from cocoa seed through fermentation, drying, roasting, nibs and nonalcoholic chocolate liquor to cocoa solids, cocoa butter and finished chocolate. Explain what conching can do to particles, moisture, volatile acids, texture and flavor, and what the generic process material cannot establish about the five exact products. Explain why a percentage does not disclose the complete recipe. Address why white chocolate can contain a cocoa-derived fat while lacking nonfat cocoa solids, why a universal real-or-fake slogan is too broad, and why ruby should remain definition- and evidence-bounded rather than being accepted or rejected from a seller title.

Then specify the smallest workable tasting. Verify the current exact packages and guest constraints first. Keep unlike-category exploration separate from any matched comparison. Where samples are actually comparable, hide brand, package, price and marketing, use equal coded portions, randomize or counterbalance order, standardize serving temperature, and record aroma, sweetness, bitterness, acidity, texture, melt, aftertaste, detected difference and overall liking as separate observations. Keep all conclusions local to these guests, products, lots and conditions.

Finish with a transparent pass, fail or unresolved decision procedure using predeclared guest count, total budget, minimum useful category coverage, serving size, acceptance threshold and safety stops. Recommend only the lowest-commitment exact lineup that clears identity, allergen, budget, category-coverage and local-acceptance gates. If none does, use a verified known control, reduce the lineup or defer. Do not name a universal product, percentage, process or category winner.
